speechtext:
It is not because some of the county court judges are not earning fairly good salaries to-day that I advocate an increase in their pay. but because they are earning a portion of their income in other ways besides dealing with cases in court.

There are but few county court judges who do not get more than $2,400 in the aggregate. 1 do not know of any who do not. The provincial government has passed various laws, particularly municipal laws, under which the services of county court judges are called upon and remunerated. If you want to readjust a school section, you call in a county court judge as arbitrator. Many of these provincial laws are so formed as to provide work for these judges. They get twenty dollars per day in arbitration cases, and it does not take long at that rate to run up $100. This is giving a premium to a judge to try and get that kind of work, and he will sometimes do it at the expenses of his proper work. There are many ways, under the provincial statutes, in which a county court judge can earn money outside his salary. That ought not to be. A judge ought to be paid fair remuneration for his work and thus made entirely independent of other sources. During the past few years, our provincial legislature has been legislating in such a direction as to bring the judges under their control, by providing a portion of their salaries from various provincial sources. I am not stating it as fact, but it is a current rumour that this is done in order to give the provincial government control of the judges. If that be the case it is to be regretted. That should not be the case. Now." if the salaries of these judges are not sufficient for them to live on-and I frankly admit that if they are only paid $2,400 a year I do not think they could live on it as judges should live-the matter should be dealt with. We are told that these judges are earning from other sources. As I have said, if that be the fact, it only strengthens the argument that their salaries should be increased. But in that case, I would favour paying them full and fair compensation ;
I would not object even if it were a little large compared with the earnings of men in other walks of life. Give them fair compensation, but pay it from some one source, and make them entirely independent of political parties or individual corporations or any power that may influence them judicially. That should not be allowed ; and that is the reason why I advocate increasing their salaries, and also why I advocate paying them from some one source.
